# The address the RTOS currently stops on.
#
# smoke.sh compares the first stop against this. A stop anywhere else is
# news: either the crank moved forward, in which case update this file in the
# same change, or a model that used to work has stopped working. While this
# line holds an address, smoke.sh skips the Linux check -- a refused access
# stops the whole machine, so CPU3 never gets its chance, and that is a fact
# about where the RTOS has got to rather than anything wrong with Linux.
# Clear the line when the crank runs out of blockers.

frontier 0x015540000

# Superseded in the same round: with the image program modelled the RTOS no
# longer stops at all in 45 s (smoke.sh reports "nothing stopped"). The line
# stays because clearing it turns on the Linux check, and Linux in the full
# machine is known broken for a reason that is now understood: the RTOS
# starts CPU3 itself (sub_40C9CD68 signals it through WorkSRAM 0x082013e0
# and only then builds share_mem), so the board's li-wait-ipc stand-in
# hijacks a core that is already on its way into Linux -- CPU3 ends at
# 0x200 with VBAR_EL1 still zero. Clear this once that hand-off is fixed.

# Recorded with SNOR_USER set (smoke.sh). Without the per-unit SNOR_USER
# body the RTOS never gets here: configuration object 28 reads 0, a mask
# loop in sub_40321450 runs 2^32 times, and the module lifecycle's barrier
# desynchronises -- see "Status, NAND round" in docs/plan.md.

